ked at me  and he said that we needed to get  control to get control of that child so  how did that make you feel defeated Amy  couldn't let it go so she took to social  media  launching this tweet treated very poorly  by the queue security last night for  Autism Awareness night a message that  reverberated throughout the arena I felt  like I you know missed an opportunity on  a professional level you know for us to  be better than not Amy's complaint got  the attention of Antony Bonavita senior  vice president for facility operations  at the Q the Bellis experienced hit home  for Bonavita his son Dominic is also on  the autism spectrum you know a personal  level if I got him back ultimately he  falls on me Bona Vita reached out to the  Bella's family what did they say when  they reached out of course initially  apologizing but then they were they just  listened and I just gave them some  suggestions the things that I was  thinking of I wanted to make sure they  were gonna follow through this really  needs to be done this can't happen again  the Q responded quickly working with the  Cleveland Clinic on training for staff  the goal to become more inclusive for  those with sensory issues ranging from  autism to PTSD why was that so important  for the Q you know for any business you  want to be as broad as you can to  attract as many different people as you  can that's just good business but we're  more than that what we mean to this city  means more than a business element we  want it to be more about my son or your  song or anybody else's parent or  somebody has PTSD to know that they have  a home and a place that they can come  now when a family like the bella says  comes to the q they can pick up a  sensory friendly bag it includes items  like fidget toys a weighted lap pad and  noise cancelling headphones there's also  a sensory room a designated space away  from the action that serves as an escape  if the sights and sounds in the arena  get to be too much compressing a moat  but inside soothing bubble art on the  walls  and tactile displays that can calm a  mind that's been overstimulated even as  we were walking right now you can hear  how loud the traffic in the queue can  thank Michelle Kong for the changes she  and her husband Julian Maha are  physicians and the parents of two boys  including a 10 year old who is autistic  they created a nonprofit called culture  city focused on a mission of inclusion  we saw how having a diagnosis like  autism it truly made the families  isolated from the community so the  genesis of culture city really was us  saying how can we change the culture of  a community to one where it is inclusive  to all individuals regardless of their  challenges culture city started by  helping their local zoo in Birmingham  Alabama becomes sensory inclusive in  2014 but perhaps their biggest impact  has been sports venues culture city has  helped 15 NBA and NHL arenas and one NFL  stadium becomes sensory aware their  first was the Q so what's it like now  coming to games with the sensory  awareness room you feel welcome to sit  and look around and know that we can do  what other families can do this is  pretty awesome and it really is minimal  cost for those rooms and the idea that  maybe just one more fan can go to a  concert or go to a game it's all the  difference and you read the emails from  these people that were able to it's  incredible good for them  those kids deserve a shot at seeing some  sports in an arena I think we all need a  room with soothing Bubba we do we do
 

